# {{name}}

An Android application built with [PyMobile](https://github.com/Maksum867/PyMobile).
Run the commands below from this project directory, in the Python environment
where PyMobile is installed. If `pymobile` is not on your PATH,
`python -m pymobile` uses the active Python interpreter instead.

## Project files

- `main.py` — the starter app; edit this to change the screen and behaviour.
- `pymobile.toml` — app name, package, permissions, build options and output folder.
- `pymobile.schema.json` — configuration schema for editor assistance.
- `assets/` — a place for app images and other packaged assets.
- `.gitignore` — files that should stay out of version control.

## Preview and edit

```bash
python -m pymobile run          # text preview
python -m pymobile run --web    # interactive preview in a local browser
python -m pymobile dev          # desktop window with reload on save
python -m pymobile check-ui     # accessibility and narrow-layout advisories
```

The text and browser previews do not need the Android SDK. The GUI needs Tk and
an available display; `dev` falls back to text watching if it cannot open one.
The browser preview listens on loopback by default. Only run trusted app code:
previewing executes the entry point and is not a sandbox.

## Check the project

```bash
python -m pymobile doctor       # basic environment and project checks
python -m pymobile check        # check project and desktop startup
python -m pymobile info         # resolved configuration and output path
```

`doctor` does not execute the app or guarantee that a build will work.
`check` runs trusted app startup on a desktop stub; neither check replaces
Android-device testing.

## Build an APK

A real installable APK requires the Android toolchain. When you are ready,
`setup-sdk` downloads it; check the destination and available disk space first.

```bash
python -m pymobile setup-sdk
python -m pymobile doctor --strict
python -m pymobile build --native
```

The default output directory is `apk/`, not `build/`. If `output_dir` was
changed in `pymobile.toml`, use that location instead; `pymobile info` shows the
resolved output path. `python -m pymobile build` without `--native` produces a
structural package that is **not installable** on Android.

To install a native APK on an authorized connected device, run
`python -m pymobile install`; see the [installation and troubleshooting guide](https://github.com/Maksum867/PyMobile#installing-on-a-phone)
for USB setup, multiple devices and logs. Debug signing is for development;
keep release keystores and passwords private.

